 # GroupFlow
 
-Also called: Groupwise SE(3) Motion Distillation
+Also called: Groupwise SE(3) Motion Distillation, Temporal Motion Grouping
 
 ## Overview
-GroupFlow is a motion compression technique for dynamic 3D Gaussian Splatting that replaces per-Gaussian neural deformation fields with shared SE(3) transformations for groups of Gaussians [S1, unofficial]. It solves the computational bottleneck of evaluating individual deformation networks for every primitive at every timestep by distilling local motion coherence into a smaller set of group-level rigid-body trajectories [S1, unofficial]. Unlike sibling methods that may use global rigid models or dense neural fields, GroupFlow adapts to local motion patterns via clustering, offering a trade-off between the fidelity of neural fields and the speed of rigid body approximations [S1, unofficial].
+GroupFlow is a motion representation technique for dynamic 3D Gaussian Splatting that replaces per-Gaussian neural deformation fields with shared SE(3) transformations for groups of Gaussians [S1, unofficial]. It solves the computational bottleneck of evaluating individual deformation networks for every primitive at every timestep, accelerating rendering and training while maintaining temporal coherence [S1, unofficial]. Unlike methods that retain dense neural fields or use global rigid transforms, GroupFlow captures locally coherent motion by clustering Gaussians based on trajectory similarity and fitting a separate SE(3) trajectory to each cluster [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
-GroupFlow operates on a set of 3D Gaussians $\mathcal{G}$, each represented by a sequence of mean positions $\mathcal{M}_i = \{\mu_i^t\}_{t=0}^{F-1}$ across $F$ timesteps [S1, unofficial]. The method consists of three main phases: initialization and clustering, trajectory fitting, and deformation prediction [S1, unofficial].
+GroupFlow operates on a dynamic scene represented by a set of 3D Gaussians $\mathcal{G} = \{g_1, \dots, g_N\}$, where each Gaussian $g_i$ has a time-varying mean position $\mu_i^t$, rotation $r_i^t$, and scale $s_i^t$ [S1, unofficial]. The method distills the learned deformation field into grouped SE(3) transformations [S1, unofficial].
 
